<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::trade_flow_disruption" target="ai_governance_cyber_risk::ai_investment_flow" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">Persistent disruption to chip, server and optical-equipment supply chains raises hardware scarcity and deployment risk, causing investors to delay or withdraw funding from hardware-intensive AI projects and infrastructure builds.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d39">ai_governance_cyber_risk</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::trade_flow_disruption" target="ai_governance_cyber_risk::market_concentration" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.65</data>
  <data key="d36">Disrupted international logistics and component shortages disproportionately harm smaller AI firms while large incumbents with diversified supply chains and capital reserves capture market share, increasing concentration.</data>
  <data key="d37">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d38">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d39">ai_governance_cyber_risk</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::trade_flow_disruption" target="global_energy_volatility::export_flow_volume" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.9</data>
  <data key="d36">Physical interruptions to ports, blocked shipping lanes, and logistics bottlenecks directly prevent crude and product cargoes from being loaded and delivered, reducing the actual volume exported.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d39">global_energy_volatility</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::trade_flow_disruption" target="iran_hormuz_standoff::maritime_throughput_rate" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.7</data>
  <data key="d36">Disruptions to Black Sea and Russian exports force Europe and buyers to source more oil and bulk cargoes from Gulf suppliers, increasing the volume of merchant traffic transiting the Strait of Hormuz as shipments are re-routed to meet shortfalls.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d39">iran_hormuz_standoff</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::trade_flow_disruption" target="iran_hormuz_standoff::coercive_leverage" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">Sustained interruptions to grain and energy flows from the Russia–Ukraine war tighten global markets, raising the strategic value of threats to disrupt Hormuz transit and thereby increasing Iran's bargaining leverage in seeking concessions or sanctions relief.</data>
  <data key="d37">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d38">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d39">iran_hormuz_standoff</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::trade_flow_disruption" target="us_china_tech_rivalry::critical_materials_avail" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.8</data>
  <data key="d36">Blockades, port damage and sanction-driven export bans and rerouting from Russia/Ukraine directly reduce shipments of palladium, neon, titanium and other critical inputs, shrinking accessible global supplies.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_tech_rivalry</data>
</edge>
