<edge source="global_energy_volatility::global_oil_price_level" target="ai_governance_cyber_risk::ai_investment_flow" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.7</data>
  <data key="d36">Sharp increases in oil prices raise electricity and logistics costs for data centers and hardware suppliers, compressing developer and corporate margins and prompting near-term cuts or delays in capital deployment to AI projects.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">global_energy_volatility</data>
  <data key="d39">ai_governance_cyber_risk</data>
</edge>
<edge source="global_energy_volatility::global_oil_price_level" target="ai_governance_cyber_risk::cyber_risk_prevalence" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">Sustained oil price spikes compress corporate and public budgets, encouraging cuts to cybersecurity staffing and patching programs while also incentivizing opportunistic state/nonstate actors to target energy and high-value tech infrastructure, increasing cyber incident frequency and severity.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">global_energy_volatility</data>
  <data key="d39">ai_governance_cyber_risk</data>
</edge>
<edge source="global_energy_volatility::global_oil_price_level" target="iran_hormuz_standoff::coercive_leverage" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.8</data>
  <data key="d36">A rise in global oil prices increases the economic damage that a disruption of flows through Hormuz would impose on importers, raising Tehran's bargaining value when it threatens or times harassment of transit.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">global_energy_volatility</data>
  <data key="d39">iran_hormuz_standoff</data>
</edge>
<edge source="global_energy_volatility::global_oil_price_level" target="russia_ukraine_war::escalation_pressure" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.7</data>
  <data key="d36">Sustained rises in global oil prices raise Russia's real-time export receipts and budget flexibility even at stable volumes, enabling political leaders and commanders to finance operations and sustain losses, which increases political and operational pressure to pursue stronger military options rather than de‑escalate.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">global_energy_volatility</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
<edge source="global_energy_volatility::global_oil_price_level" target="russia_ukraine_war::air_defense_capacity" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">Persistently higher oil prices increase export revenue and foreign-currency availability, allowing faster procurement of spare parts, ammunition and contracted maintenance for air‑defence systems, thereby raising operational availability over the medium term.</data>
  <data key="d37">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d38">global_energy_volatility</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
