<edge source="global_energy_volatility::maritime_transit_capacit" target="ai_governance_cyber_risk::market_concentration" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">Higher usable transit capacity lowers logistical bottlenecks and entry barriers for smaller AI vendors, whereas constrained capacity advantages vertically integrated hyperscalers that can internalize logistics and inventories, so increases in capacity tend to reduce concentration.</data>
  <data key="d37">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d38">global_energy_volatility</data>
  <data key="d39">ai_governance_cyber_risk</data>
</edge>
<edge source="global_energy_volatility::maritime_transit_capacit" target="russia_ukraine_war::trade_flow_disruption" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.7</data>
  <data key="d36">A reduction in available maritime transit capacity (through chokepoint congestion, convoy restrictions or port closures) immediately lowers throughput and creates bottlenecks that amplify and prolong trade disruptions affecting Ukrainian exports and regional shipping.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">global_energy_volatility</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
<edge source="global_energy_volatility::maritime_transit_capacit" target="us_china_tech_rivalry::critical_materials_avail" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.85</data>
  <data key="d36">A sudden reduction in maritime transit capacity through chokepoints directly delays and reduces shipments of mined and processed critical minerals and precursors, lowering the immediately accessible supply in importing tech manufacturing hubs.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">global_energy_volatility</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_tech_rivalry</data>
</edge>
